  + Prove that if $G$ is a connected Lie group with Lie algebra $frak(g)$ then the group commutant $[G,G]$ (the subgroup of $G$ generated by elements $g h g^(-1) h^(-1)$, $g,h in G$) is a Lie subgroup of $G$ with Lie algebra $[frak(g),frak(g)]$.
  + Let $tilde(G) = RR times H$, where $H$ is the #emph[Heisenberg Group] of real matrices of the form 
    $ M(a,b,c):= mat(1,a,b;0,1,c;0,0,1), quad a,b,c in RR $
    Let $Gamma tilde.equiv ZZ^2 subset tilde(G)$ be the (closed) central subgroup generated by the pairs $(1,M(0,0,0) = id)$ and $(sqrt(2), M(0,1,0))$. Let $G = tilde(G)\/Gamma$. Show that $[G,G]$ is not closed in $G$ (although by (i) it is a Lie subgroup).

  + Does $[G,G]$ have to be closed in $G$ if $G$ is simply-connected? (Consider $Hom(G,RR)$ and apply the second fundamental theorem of Lie theory).
][
  + WLOG, we'll take the universal cover $tilde(G)$ instead of $G$, since the covering map $p:tilde(G) arrow.r G$ is a local diffeomorphism satisfying $p([tilde(G),tilde(G)]) = [G,G]$ ($[tilde(G),tilde(G)]$ is a subgroup generated by the paths of the form $[x(t) y(t) x(t)^(-1) y(t)^(-1)]$, which gets mapped down to $x(1)y(1)x(1)^(-1)y(1)^(-1) in [G,G]$. Since $G$ is connected, $x(1),y(1)$ can be arbitrary, so the generators of $[G,G]$ is contained in $p([tilde(G),tilde(G)])$, hence $[G,G] subset.eq p([tilde(G),tilde(G)])$; the other direction is given by the fact that every generator of $[tilde(G),tilde(G)]$ is mapped into $[G,G]$, hence $p([tilde(G),tilde(G)]) subset.eq [G,G]$). Then, if proving that $[tilde(G),tilde(G)]$ is a submanifold corresponding to $[frak(g),frak(g)]$, it follows from the covering map that $[G,G] = p([tilde(G),tilde(G)])$ is also a submanifold corresponding to $[frak(g),frak(g)]$ (since the evenly covered neighborhood of $1 in G$ and $[1] in tilde(G)$ shows that their tangent space must be isomorphic via the differential of covering map $p_*$, hence also isomorphic as Lie Algebra).

    To comprehend this, on the Lie algebra level, we have the following "exact sequence" (where $pi$ is the quotient map):
    #set align(center)
    #diagram($
              0 edge(->) & [frak(g),frak(g)] edge("hook->") & frak(g) edge(->>, script(pi)) & frak(g)\/[frak(g),frak(g)] edge(->) & 0
            $)
    #set align(left)
    Using Fundamental Theorem of Lie Theory (equivalence of categories between simply-connected Lie groups and Lie algebras), take the simply-connected Lie group of $frak(g)$ (which $tilde(G)$ satisfies this), and $tilde(K)$ of $frak(g)\/[frak(g),frak(g)]$. The Second Fundamental Theorem of Lie Theory states the bijection between $Hom(tilde(G),tilde(K)) arrow.r.tilde Hom(frak(g),frak(g)\/[frak(g),frak(g)])$ by sending the Lie group homomorphism $phi mapsto phi_*$, hence for the quotient map $pi:frak(g) arrow.r frak(g)\/[frak(g),frak(g)]$, there exists a unique Lie group homomorphism $phi:tilde(G) arrow.r tilde(K)$, such that $phi_*=pi$. Which, let $H:=ker(phi)$ be a Lie subgroup of $tilde(G)$, it forms the following correspondance of exact sequences:
    #set align(center)
    #diagram($
              0 edge(->) & H edge("hook->") & tilde(G) edge(->>, script(phi)) & tilde(K) edge(->) & 0\ 
              0 edge(->) & [frak(g),frak(g)] edge("hook->") & frak(g) edge("u",->, script(exp_(tilde(G)))) edge(->>, script(pi=phi_*)) & frak(g)\/[frak(g),frak(g)] edge("u",->, script(exp_(tilde(K)))) edge(->) & 0
