#problem[
  Etingof Lecture Notes Exercise 15.16:

  Prove Proposition 15.15:
  #proposition[
    Any Lie subalgebra or quotient of a nilpotent Lie algebra is nilpotent.
  ][]
][
  First, suppose $frak(g)$ is a nilpotent Lie algebra, with $frak(h) subset.eq frak(g)$ being a subalgebra. To show the nilpotency of $frak(h)$, it suffices to show its Lower Central Series is always contained in $frak(g)$'s Lower Central Series.

  For $n=1$, it's clear that $D_1(frak(h))=[frak(h),frak(h)]subset.eq [frak(g),frak(g)]=D_1(frak(g))$ (for proof cf. #text(weight: "bold")[Problem 3 part (i)]).

  Now, suppose for given $n in NN$, it satisfies $D_(n-1)(frak(h)) subset.eq D_(n-1)(frak(g))$. Then for $D_(n)(frak(h)) = [D_(n-1)(frak(h)),frak(h)]$, since all its generators are of the form $[x,y]$, where $x in D_(n-1)(frak(h)) subset.eq D_(n-1)(frak(g))$ and $y in frak(h) subset.eq frak(g)$, so $[x,y] in [D_(n-1)(frak(g)),frak(g)] = D_(n)(frak(g))$. Hence, all generators of $D_(n)(frak(h))$ is contained in $D_(n)(frak(g))$, showing $D_(n)(frak(h)) subset.eq D_(n)(frak(g))$. Hence, by induction all $n in NN$ satisfies $D_(n)(frak(h)) subset.eq D_(n)(frak(g))$.

  As a result, since $frak(g)$ is nilpotent, there exists $k in NN$, where $D_(k)(frak(g))=0$, and it enforces $D_(k)(frak(h))=0$ also, so $frak(h)$ is nilpotent.

  \ 

  Now, given an ideal $I subset.eq frak(g)$, and the quotient map $pi:frak(g) arrow.r.twohead frak(g)\/I$ (for simplicity again, denote $frak(g)\/I = frak(k)$). We'll show its Lower Central Series is always contained in the image of $frak(g)$'s Lower Central Series.

  For $n=1$,it's also clear that $D_1(frak(k)) = D^1(frak(k)) subset.eq pi(D^1(frak(g))) = pi(D_1(frak(g)))$ (again, cf. #text(weight: "bold")[Problem 3 part (i)]).

  Now, suppose for given $n in NN$, it satisfies $D_(n-1)(frak(k)) subset.eq pi(D_(n-1)(frak(g)))$, then for $D_(n)(frak(k)) = [D_(n-1)(frak(k)),frak(k)]$, the generators are of the form $[overline(x),overline(y)]$, where $overline(x) in D_(n-1)(frak(k)) subset.eq pi(D_(n-1)(frak(g)))$ (say corresponds to the image of $x in D_(n-1)(frak(g))$), and $overline(y) in frak(k)$ (say corresponds to the image of $y in frak(g)$). Then, since $[overline(x),overline(y)] = overline([x,y])$, where $[x,y] in [D_(n-1)(frak(g)),frak(g)] = D_(n)(frak(g))$, showing that $[overline(x),overline(y)] in pi(D_(n)(frak(g)))$. Hence, $D_(n)(frak(k)) subset.eq pi(D_(n)(frak(g)))$ (since all generators satisfy this relation). Therefore, by induction every $n in NN$ satisfies $D_(n)(frak(k)) subset.eq pi(D_(n)(frak(g)))$.


  As a result, since $frak(g)$ is nilpotent, there exists $k in NN$ where $D_(k)(frak(g))=0$, which enforces $D_(k)(frak(k))=0$ also (since $pi(D_(k)(frak(g)))=0$). So, $frak(k)$ is also nilpotent.
